data: {"id":"chatcmpl_d0fb75f5-b12c-4e52-99ba-f6cec8b55c80","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792089427,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"Referee assessmentC1 is a special_case_of_known, not an open conjecture. Its conditional proof escape is bind_only relative to the inspected baseline. The relevant published result is the first-term upper bound in the classical Bonferroni inequalities for the number of events occurring. Institute of Statistical Mathematics+1No PDF was accessible in the current conversation. Targeted Library searches also did not retrieve a matching source. Accordingly, this report evaluates the supplied C1 text, not an unseen manuscript.Well-posedness and scopeThe excerpt does not explicitly define the listing space containing  $s$ , the assignment of  $q$ -values to cells, or the predicate “row equals the  $f$ -twisted diagonal.” These omissions prevent a fully self-contained, model-specific statement. I record well_posed: false for that presentation defect, not because the underlying inequality is false.The mathematical core is nevertheless sufficiently clear to classify: a finite listing space, nonnegative weights, and a nonnegative integer-valued count. The particular row predicate cannot create an additional hypothesis for this counting bound; the inspected baseline allows arbitrary event families. Institute of Statistical MathematicsThe statement should specify universal quantification over the finite sets,  $q$ ,  $f$ , and  $k$ , together with the conventions\n$$\\binom{c}{0}=1,\\qquad \\binom{c}{k}=0\\ \\text{when }k\u003ec.$$\nIt should also specify empty sums and products. No normalization of the cell weights is stated and none should silently be added.Opened baseline and conditional proof contentThe decisive source is Bonferroni-type inequalities; Chebyshev-type inequalities for the distributions on [0, n] [opened: https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/043_2_0261.pdf]. Lemma 2.2 identifies intersection sums with binomial moments; Proposition 6.1, equation (6.2), supplies the required upper bound at  $u=0$ . Institute of Statistical Mathematics+2The source-to-claim matching is recorded in the JSON below. It consists of substituting the row predicates, identifying their count with  $C$ , and matching the weighted sums through total-mass normalization when that mass is positive. Zero total mass and the boundary values of  $k$  require separate convention checks, not an additional model-dependent estimate.A corroborating source is Elementary Tail Bounds on the Hypergeometric Distribution [opened: arXiv:2510.19726], §2, equations (10)–(11), which records the Markov/factorial-moment formulation. Its distribution-specific results are not being applied to the listing model. arXivThus, the bind_only assessment rests on an inspected matching result—not on missing proof evidence or an assumption that elementary mathematics cannot contain new content. C1 asks for no strengthening beyond that baseline. This conclusion concerns the stated obligation; no submitted proof has been checked. Institute of Statistical Mathematics+1Utility, information gain, and feedbackThe assertion has ordinary utility as a reusable upper bound. That utility is inherited from the known theorem and should not be confused with a new contribution. Nothing supplied establishes an efficient way to evaluate  $B_k$ , a computational improvement, or a separate application.No explicit information-gain model is supplied, and no named open-problem settlement is claimed. Both corresponding objects remain null.Recommendation: revise the display as an explicitly defined, cited known proposition or corollary, rather than an open question. A stronger claim would need to state precisely what additional model-dependent conclusion is sought; merely removing independence or changing notation would not establish a new obligation. This is presentation feedback, not a publication decision.JSON{\n  \"recommendation\": \"revise\",\n  \"summary\": \"C1's finite nonnegative-weight counting assertion is a special case of a known Bonferroni/binomial-moment bound. Its conditional obligation is bind_only relative to the inspected baseline. The supplied wording is not self-contained at the listing-model level, and no matching PDF was accessible. Revise it as a cited known proposition rather than an open conjecture.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"The nonnegativity hypothesis identifies the relevant finite-measure setting.\",\n    \"A directly matching published baseline was located and its mathematical statements were inspected.\",\n    \"The assertion has ordinary reusable upper-bound utility, independently of novelty.\"\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": [\n    \"The listing space, exact cell-weight product, and f-twisted-diagonal row predicate are not explicitly defined in the supplied excerpt.\",\n    \"Presenting the assertion as open would misrepresent its known mathematical status.\",\n    \"No model-dependent strengthening or computational advantage is asserted or demonstrated.\"\n  ],\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"correctness\": \"not_checked\",\n      \"shape\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": [],\n      \"known\": [\n        \"Bonferroni-type inequalities; Chebyshev-type inequalities for the distributions on [0, n] [opened: https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/043_2_0261.pdf]\",\n        \"Elementary Tail Bounds on the Hypergeometric Distribution [opened: arXiv:2510.19726]\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"Correctness denotes the absence of a submitted-proof audit, not uncertainty about the abstract bound's known status. The source-dependent wording needs definitions, but every interpretation satisfying the described finite nonnegative-weight/count hypotheses has the same literature classification. Conditional escape is assessed against an inspected theorem, with no live proof witnesses. General-theorem utility is supported only as inherited utility; computational utility and a new contribution are not established. Information gain and settlement are unclaimed.\",\n      \"conjecture\": {\n        \"well_posed\": false,\n        \"well_posed_reason\": \"As a standalone source-specific statement, C1 leaves the domain of s, the exact product defining w(s), and the f-twisted-diagonal row predicate unspecified. The unavailable PDF prevents checking those definitions. The intended abstract assertion is unambiguous once one specifies a finite listing space, nonnegative real weights, and C taking values in {0,...,|A|}. State universal quantification over finite A,Y, q:A×Y→R, f:Y→Y and k in the nonnegative integers; specify binomial and empty-sum/product conventions. No probability normalization or extra property of f should be silently imposed.\",\n        \"status\": \"special_case_of_known\",\n        \"status_reason\": \"The intended bound matches the u=0 case of Proposition 6.1, equation (6.2), together with Lemma 2.2 in the decisive opened source.\",\n        \"named_works\": [\n          \"Bonferroni-type inequalities; Chebyshev-type inequalities for the distributions on [0, n] [opened: https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/043_2_0261.pdf]\",\n          \"Elementary Tail Bounds on the Hypergeometric Distribution [opened: arXiv:2510.19726]\"\n        ],\n        \"escape\": \"bind_only\",\n        \"escape_reason\": \"For the stated scalar inequality, the inspected baseline already supplies the needed conclusion after matching the row events and count, translating finite weights to probability masses when total mass is positive, and resolving boundary conventions. The listing structure and f contribute no additional estimate. This is a conditional statement-level assessment, not a classification of an unseen proof.\",\n        \"suggestions\": [\n          \"Define the listing space and give explicit formulas for w(s), the twisted diagonal, the qualifying-row predicate, and C(s), without relying on an inaccessible source.\",\n          \"Specify k=0, k\u003e|A|, empty sets, zero weights, empty products, and the standard binomial conventions.\",\n          \"Present C1 as a known proposition or corollary with the exact baseline citation.\",\n          \"For any proposed strengthening, state an additional model-sensitive bound or evaluation result precisely and compare it against the existing moment bounds; do not present removal of independence or a renamed wrapper as new content.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"methodol"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_d0fb75f5-b12c-4e52-99ba-f6cec8b55c80","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792089427,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"ogy\": {\n        \"baseline\": [\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"A and Y are finite; q:A×Y→R and f:Y→Y; every q(a,y) is nonnegative. The described listings are finite, w(s) is a product of |A|² cell weights, and C(s) counts qualifying rows among |A| rows.\",\n            \"location\": \"Supplied CONFIRMED STATEMENTS, C1.statement, opening model-description sentence and final nonnegativity clause; duplicated in SOURCE CONTEXT. No manuscript PDF location was available.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"T_k is the sum of w(s) over listings with C(s)≥k, and B_k is the sum of binom(C(s),k)w(s) over all listings.\",\n            \"location\": \"Supplied CONFIRMED STATEMENTS, C1.statement, sentence beginning 'Define T_k'; duplicated in SOURCE CONTEXT.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For n≥1 arbitrary events E_i on a probability space, N=Σ_i 1_{E_i} and S_r=Σ_{|I|=r}P(∩_{i∈I}E_i)=E[binom(N,r)]. If 1≤m≤n and u is a nonnegative integer with 2u≤n-m, then P(N≥m)≤Σ_{r=m}^{m+2u}(-1)^(r-m)binom(r-1,m-1)S_r.\",\n            \"location\": \"Equation (1.1), printed p. 261; Lemma 2.2, printed p. 263; Proposition 6.1, equation (6.2) and its parameter restrictions, printed pp. 278–279. PDF pages 1, 3, 18 and 19 were visually inspected.\",\n            \"source\": \"Bonferroni-type inequalities; Chebyshev-type inequalities for the distributions on [0, n] [opened: https://www.ism.ac.jp/editsec/aism/pdf/043_2_0261.pdf]\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"searched\": [\n          \"Conversation Files search: 'bonferroni-upper finite independent listing model nonnegative cell weights number of rows equal f-twisted diagonal T_k B_k'; the tool reported no current-conversation files.\",\n          \"Targeted Library searches used 'bonferroni-upper', the supplied finite-listing description, and 'f-twisted diagonal' with 'cell weights'; no matching source was retrieved in the inspected results. These were not exhaustive Library listings.\",\n          \"General web queries: 'binomial moment inequality P(X \u003e= k) E binom X k'; 'Bonferroni inequalities probability at least r events binomial moments upper bound S_r'; '\\\"binomial moment\\\" \\\"upper bound\\\" \\\"at least\\\"'; '\\\"Bonferroni\\\" \\\"at least r\\\"'; '\\\"factorial moment\\\" \\\"Markov\\\" inequality'.\",\n          \"Follow-up web queries: '\\\"Comparison between the Chernoff and Factorial Moment\\\" pdf'; '\\\"Bonferroni Inequalities\\\" Galambos 1977 pdf'; '\\\"factorial moment bound\\\" pdf'.\",\n          \"Decisive source inspection used the Institute of Statistical Mathematics journal PDF, including the event definitions, binomial-moment identity, and exact upper-bound statement and restrictions.\",\n          \"Corroborating inspection: Elementary Tail Bounds on the Hypergeometric Distribution [opened: arXiv:2510.19726], version 2, section 2, equations (10)–(11). Its hypergeometric-specific propositions were not used as prerequisites.\",\n          \"Publisher-access attempts included Project Euclid, JSTOR, and Taylor \u0026 Francis. Those attempts did not expose usable theorem text and supplied no decisive evidence. No exhaustive specialist-database or forward-citation search was performed.\"\n        ],\n        \"literature\": \"supported\",\n        \"binding_analysis\": \"The parameter matching is n=|A|, m=k, u=0. Each E_a is the supplied qualifying-row predicate, so its occurrence count is C. Write Z=Σ_s w(s). For Z\u003e0, the normalized masses are w(s)/Z, and the source quantities match T_k/Z and B_k/Z; rescaling removes Z. Nonnegativity of the product weights is an assumption-substitution and elementary positivity-closure check. Z=0, k=0, k\u003e|A| and empty-index cases are separate zero/empty-sum and binomial-convention normalizations. No independence assumption, property of f, new counting identity, or model-dependent estimate remains in the scalar bound. This records reference matching, not a submitted proof or a new proof construction.\",\n        \"delta\": \"There is no additional scalar bound beyond the inspected baseline under the stated finite nonnegative-weight interpretation. The remaining source-fidelity obligation is to provide and check the omitted listing, weight, and row-predicate definitions, followed by the specified parameter matching and boundary conventions. No stronger inequality, new construction, or computational guarantee is part of C1.\",\n        \"escape\": \"bind_only\",\n        \"witnesses\": [],\n        \"utility\": {\n          \"kind\": \"general_theorem\",\n          \"status\": \"supported\",\n          \"rationale\": \"The assertion provides a reusable tail-mass upper bound. This is ordinary utility inherited from a known theorem, not evidence of novelty or an independent computational contribution. A general theorem needs no artificial downstream consumer, but nothing here establishes that computing B_k is easier than computing T_k.\",\n          \"problem\": \"For every described finite listing model with nonnegative cell weights and every nonnegative integer k, is the weight of listings with at least k qualifying rows at most the kth weighted binomial moment?\",\n          \"progress\": \"The abstract question is already answered affirmatively by the inspected baseline. No additional open-problem progress or separate application is established.\",\n          \"prerequisites\": \"A finite listing space; nonnegative real cell weights; listing weights given by finite products of those weights; a count C in {0,...,|A|}; the displayed definitions of T_k and B_k; standard binomial and empty-sum/product conventions.\",\n          \"discharge\": \"The finite-model and nonnegativity premises are supplied as hypotheses, not experimentally verified facts. The opened baseline supplies the generic estimate after the recorded matching. Exact agreement with the source-specific definitions remains not_checkable because no matching manuscript was accessible. No submitted proof or computational discharge was audited.\"\n        },\n        \"settlement\": null,\n        \"information_gain\": null,\n        \"limits\": [\n          \"The well-posedness flag records missing standalone definitions; it does not classify the intended inequality as false.\",\n          \"The literature comparison supports the abstract scalar assertion, not an audit of an unseen manuscript or its model definitions.\",\n          \"Inaccessible publisher content was treated as not_checkable and was not used to assert a theorem or historical priority.\",\n          \"No proof was submitted: correctness is not_checked, proof shape is null, and there are no live witnesses.\",\n          \"The utility finding concerns inherited general-theorem utility only; new mathematical value and computational improvement are not established.\",\n          \"No explicit information-gain model or measurement was supplied, and no named open-problem settlement was claimed.\",\n          \"Sources are model-reported, not venue-verified. No Lean verification, compiled registration, Trureturing certificate, or AURIC/FIB measurement is claimed.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"limits\": [\n    \"No PDF was accessible in the current conversation, and the inspected targeted Library search results did not identify a matching source. Only the supplied C1 wording was reviewed.\",\n    \"The mathematical status assessment is supported by an opened matching baseline; missing manuscript definitions remain an independent source-fidelity limitation.\",\n    \"The search was targeted, not exhaustive, and does not establish earliest historical priority.\",\n    \"Correctness, conditional proof escape, utility, information gain, and settlement were assessed separately; no combined score or escape percentage was produced.\",\n    \"The report contains a source comparison, not a new proof, counterexample, formal verification, or publication decision.\"\n  ],\n  \"text\": \"Revise C1 into a self-contained, cited known proposition or corollary. Its intended mathematical assertion is a special_case_of_known and conditionally bind_only relative to the inspected baseline. The missing source definitions are an evidence limitation, while the known-status finding is affirmative mathematical evidence against prese"},"finish_reason":null}]}

